; sr04_range.pe -- HC-SR04 ultrasonic ranging: a 10 us trigger, the echo pulse
; width measured to the microsecond, and that width converted to millimetres.
;
; THE PROTOCOL, and it is the only act here whose ANSWER is a number rather
; than a waveform. A 10 us pulse on TRIG makes the device emit eight 40 kHz
; bursts and then hold ECHO high for the round trip, so the WIDTH of the echo
; pulse is the distance:
;
;     t (us) = 2 * d (mm) / 0.343 mm/us   ->   d (mm) = t * 0.17150
;
; and the constant everybody uses is 11/64 = 0.171875, which is +0.22 % long
; against the speed of sound. That error belongs to the CONSTANT and is stated
; rather than absorbed: the arithmetic below is EXACT for 11/64.
;
; THE CONVERSION IS THE ACT, and it is where the ISA bites. No multiply, no
; divide, no carry flag -- only JZ/JNZ. Four facts decide the program.
;
; 1. A 64-BYTE TABLE IS NOT AVAILABLE. The usual trick for us*11/64 is a
;    lookup of floor(11r/64) over the low six bits. This machine has SIXTEEN
;    bytes of data memory (DMEM_BYTES = 16 in rtl/pe_soc.v, and every top
;    level passes 16) and they are all spoken for, so that is not a choice
;    being declined; it does not fit.
;
; 2. THE MULTIPLY IS NOT NEEDED. Write us = 64q + r. Then
;
;        us * 11 / 64  =  11q + 11r/64  =  11q + floor(11r/64)
;
;    EXACTLY, because 11q is an integer. So the conversion is a multiply by
;    ELEVEN of a ten-bit number, a multiply by eleven of a six-bit number, and
;    one add. The plan for this act assumed a 16x16 multiply; this identity
;    removes it, along with the division and the approximation the plan
;    expected the division to cost.
;
; 3. A MULTIPLY BY ELEVEN IS FOUR DOUBLINGS AND TWO ADDS:
;
;        x -> 2x -> 4x -> 5x -> 10x -> 11x
; 
;    (5x is 4x + x, and 10x is 5x doubled.) No loop, no counter, no second
;    temporary: the multiplicand stays in Q throughout.
; 
; 4. A DOUBLING IS THE CHEAPEST 16-BIT OPERATION THIS ISA HAS, and unlike any
;    other 16-bit add it needs NO both-zero guard. The carry out of lo+lo is
;    BIT 7 OF THE ORIGINAL LOW BYTE, so one AND with 0x80 and one JNZ is the
;    whole test; and "the result is zero" means "nothing carried", because
;    doubling zero cannot carry and is the only way to reach zero. A general
;    add of a variable is different: 0 + 0 = 0 is not a carry, so the
;    addends must be re-read to tell a wrap from a pair of zeros. That
;    asymmetry is the reason the conversion is mostly doublings.
; 
;    No 16-bit overflow is possible anywhere in the conversion, and the
;    largest value involved is 11 * (23000 >> 6) = 3949: the intermediates are
;    8q = 2872 and 10q = 3590, and the second term is 11 * 63 = 693. That is
;    recorded rather than assumed, because an unnoticed overflow in the high
;    byte is invisible from the outside -- the answer would still be a
;    plausible millimetre figure.
; 
; THE TIMEBASE is the 1 us counter read as a DIFFERENCE, exactly as in
; freqmeter.pe and for the same reason: the clear-on-read flag on port 6 is one
; bit, so a poll loop longer than a tick reports several ticks as one. Port 7
; is the 4.33 us UART half-bit tick and is not a microsecond.
; 
; THE ECHO IS CAPTURED, NOT SAMPLED, which is what makes this act safe at 4 m.
; A 23 ms echo is 1.38 M clocks, and nothing here polls per clock, stores an
; edge list, or can miss an edge: the program waits for ECHO to rise, counts
; microseconds until it falls, and the count IS the measurement. The longest
; span without a poll is the 10 us trigger, where the program's worst case is
; a few hundred clocks; the 300 us sensor response is spent in a
; two-instruction poll loop.
; 
; MEMORY. Sixteen bytes, all of them:
; 
;     0,1   T   the microsecond counter, and LATER the main term (11q).
;             The reuse is safe for a reason rather than by luck: the
;             conversion runs at the ECHO FALLING edge, by which time the
;             width is already latched in US, and the next edge of ECHO is a
;             whole measurement away. T is dead until then.
;     2,3   US  the measured echo width, microseconds
;     4     PREV  the previous ECHO level (00 or 20)
;     5     STATE what the program is waiting for
;     6,7   THE answer, millimetres, low byte first
;     8,9   spare. They were slot 1's answer until the design change below,
;            and a program that writes one answer has no reason to name a
;            second slot in its own map.
;    10     DONE the completion flag: 0 in the init, 1 at the bank. It was N,
;            the number of measurements banked, and the two-measurement
;            program needed both -- which is what let the init's zero be
;            read as a bank.
;    11     TICK the previous 1 us reading, seeded by the init. This was DONE
;            in the first draft; the init's comment on it says why the
;            answer moved here, and the reason is an alias, not a shortage.
;    12,13  Q   the multiplicand
;    14,15  ACC the small term (11r, then 11r/64)
; 
; ONE measurement per run, and that is forced rather than chosen. The first
; version of this program banked two answers, which an answer being two bytes
; and the machine having sixteen makes look free -- and it does not work,
; because the exact add needs four temporaries and sixteen bytes cannot hold
; two banked answers and four temporaries DISJOINTLY. Three arrangements were
; tried and each moved the failure rather than removing it: with the
; temporaries in 6..9 they collided with the CURRENT slot's own answer; with
; them in 2, 3 and 11 the main term's Q = us>>6 setup read a destroyed US and
; multiplied zero; and the dead-byte budget during a chain turned out to be
; dmem[11] alone. So the act measures one distance per run and parks, and
; tb_pe_soc_sr04.v runs the program TWICE, once per distance, with a full
; reset between. It is the frequency meter's shape, and the finding is that
; this machine cannot hold two banked answers and a four-temporary exact
; 16-bit add at the same time -- not that the arithmetic was wrong, which it
; was never, for all 65,536 operand pairs in two forms.
; 
; THE TRIGGER PULSE is a COUNTED delay, not a wait on the tick, so its width
; is exact in clocks: 4 * SR_TRIG_LEN + 5 clocks, and SR_TRIG_LEN = 149
; makes that exactly 601 clocks = 10.017 us. SR_TRIG_LEN and not SR_TRIG,
; which is 0x40, the PIN: loading the pin instead of the length makes a
; 261-clock pulse, which is 4*64 + 5. The +5 and not the +4 this file used to
; claim is the five instructions between the counter load and the pad's
; release -- the OUT PINOE that claims the pad, the two-instruction counter
; load, the four-clock loop iteration, the LDI that sets the ending level, and
; the OUT TXPIN that drops it -- and it was COUNTED FROM THE ASSEMBLER'S
; LISTING rather than asserted, after three attempts to derive it by eye
; failed on my own pattern-matching. The testbench pins 601 as an equality for
; the same reason it pins a number at all. The value lives in peasm's CONSTS
; table because a fitted instruction count cannot be reached by editing the
; source, and a mutation gate that cannot perturb the number a protocol
; depends on is not testing the protocol.

        ; ---- init ------------------------------------------------------
init:
        LDI   A, 0
        OUT   TXPIN, A         ; the level, low
        OUT   PINOE, A         ; and BOTH pads RELEASED. The init claims
                               ; nothing: the only claim of TRIG in this program
                               ; is inside the trigger, because a pad claimed
                               ; here would be DRIVEN between measurements --
                               ; a held level where the device expects an idle
                               ; one, and a real device reads that as a
                               ; malformed trigger.
        LDI   A, 0
        STM   0, A
        STM   2, A             ; T = 0, US = 0
        STM   4, A             ; PREV = 0, the level ECHO IDLES AT. It was
                               ; left at its reset value, which is X -- and an X
                               ; in the edge detector is not a level that does
                               ; not match: `A - X` is X, `JZ` on X is FALSE,
                               ; so the very first poll reported an EDGE. The
                               ; run then went one event out of step from the
                               ; first instruction and the state machine reached
                               ; its "the echo has ended" branch before any
                               ; echo existed. PREV's initial value is part of
                               ; the program's contract with the device.
        STM   5, A             ; state 0: trigger first
        STM   10, A
        IN    A, I2CTICK
        STM   11, A            ; the previous 1 us reading, SEEDED FROM THE
                               ; COUNTER and not from zero: seeded with zeros
                               ; the first elapsed time would be the counter's
                               ; whole age. One byte is enough because the
                               ; difference is taken mod 256 and the loop is
                               ; 1.3 us. This byte was the DONE flag in the
                               ; first draft, and ACC (14,15) held the reading
                               ; instead -- which ALIASES: the conversion
                               ; overwrites dmem[14..15] and the poll loop
                               ; reads the previous reading from there. It
                               ; happens to be harmless (the microsecond count
                               ; only measures the echo, and T is zeroed at
                               ; the echo's rising edge, so a garbage elapsed
                               ; after a conversion lands in a counter that is
                               ; about to be zeroed) -- but "harmless by
                               ; accident" is exactly the reasoning that
                               ; produced every invisible defect in this
                               ; block, so the two roles are separated and
                               ; the completion flag is the slot count.

        ; ---- the poll loop ----------------------------------------------
main:
        IN    A, I2CTICK
        LDM   X, 11
        MOV   Y, A
        MOV   A, Y
        STM   11, A
        SUB   A, X             ; now - previous: ZERO means no microsecond has
        JZ    the_pin          ; passed, and NONZERO means A IS the elapsed time
        MOV   X, A             ; (1..255). One subtraction, both jobs -- and
                                ; the borrow of it is used only as a zero test,
                                ; which is the one comparison the ISA has.
        LDM   A, 0             ; T += elapsed
        ADD   A, X
        STM   0, A
        JZ    t_carry
        JMP   state_go
t_carry:
        LDM   A, 1
        ADD   A, 1
        STM   1, A

        ; ---- ECHO, sampled on every iteration --------------------------
        ; Both levels are 00 or SR_ECHO, so A - X is zero EXACTLY when the
        ; level is unchanged: the ISA's only byte comparison, and the whole
        ; edge detector rests on it.
state_go:
        ; The STATE is dispatched HERE, once per pass, and not from inside the
        ; edge detector. The first version dispatched it from the edge path,
        ; which works exactly once: after the first bank the pads are idle and
        ; ECHO never changes again, so no edge ever arrives, the trigger is
        ; never re-entered, and the run banks one measurement and stops. A
        ; state machine whose states are only reachable through an external
        ; event is a state machine that runs once.
        LDM   A, 5
        JZ    trig_go          ; state 0: the trigger is due, unconditionally

the_pin:
        IN    A, PIN
        AND   A, SR_ECHO
        MOV   X, A
        LDM   A, 4
        SUB   A, X
        JZ    main             ; no edge
        MOV   A, X
        STM   4, A             ; the level now, whichever it is

        LDM   A, 5
        SUB   A, 1
        JZ    echo_up          ; state 1: the echo has started
        JMP   echo_down        ; state 2: the echo has ended

        ; ---- the trigger: claim TRIG, then SR_TRIG_LEN iterations of a
        ; ---- four-clock loop. The claim is HERE and not in the init, so the
        ; ---- pad is RELEASED between measurements; and the LEVEL is written
        ; ---- before the ENABLE, so the pin's rising edge is the enable's own
        ; ---- effect and not one instruction early. PINOE is a whole
        ; ---- register, so both bits are written every time: a write claiming
        ; ---- one pad is also a write giving the other away, and that family
        ; ---- has now bitten this repository five times, twice in this act.
        ; ---- The first version of this program claimed TRIG in the INIT and
        ; ---- released it at the end of the first pulse, so its trigger was a
        ; ---- side effect of the init and its pulse measured 627 clocks
        ; ---- instead of the 601 the delay is fitted to.
trig_go:
        LDI   A, SR_TRIG
        OUT   TXPIN, A
        LDI   A, SR_TRIG
        OUT   PINOE, A         ; TRIG driven, ECHO still released
        LDI   A, SR_TRIG_LEN
        STM   12, A            ; the delay counter, and SR_TRIG_LEN rather
trig_wait:                    ; than SR_TRIG: those two constants are 149 and
                               ; 0x40, the PIN and the LENGTH, and the first
                               ; version of this line used the pin. The pulse
                               ; came out 260 clocks = 4.3 us -- under the
                               ; device's 10 us minimum, and caught by the
                               ; testbench's EQUALITY against 601 clocks, which
                               ; is the whole reason that check is an equality
                               ; and not the datasheet's ">= 10 us". dmem[12] is
                               ; Q's low byte, dead until the conversion.
        LDM   A, 12
        SUB   A, 1
        STM   12, A
        JNZ   trig_wait        ; 4 clocks per iteration
        LDI   A, 0
        OUT   TXPIN, A         ; TRIG falls here: 4*149 + 5 = 601 clocks
        OUT   PINOE, A         ; and BOTH pads are RELEASED on the very next
                               ; clock, with no LDI in between: A is already 0,
                               ; so the pad is never driven at a level for a
                               ; single clock after the pulse, which is what
                               ; "TRIG is never driven while it is not
                               ; pulsing" is there to catch. This is the FIFTH
        LDI   A, 1             ; write in this repository that confused a
        STM   5, A             ; level with an enable: writing SR_TRIG to
                               ; PINOE drives TRIG LOW, which is a held level
                               ; and not an idle one. The first version of this
                               ; program did exactly that, and the testbench
                               ; caught it as "TRIG is never driven while it
                               ; is not pulsing" -- a check written for a
                               ; different act's failure, firing on this one.
        JMP   main
        JMP   main

echo_up:
        LDI   A, 0
        STM   0, A
        STM   1, A             ; T = 0 HERE, not at the trigger: the sensor's
        LDI   A, 2             ; response delay is not part of the distance.
        STM   5, A             ; and the STATE ADVANCES, which the first
        JMP   main             ; version of this line did not do: with the
                               ; state still 1, the echo's FALLING edge was
                               ; read as a second rising edge, the width was
                               ; never latched, and the run banked nothing at
                               ; all. Two states are needed to tell the two
                               ; edges of the same pulse apart, and the state
                               ; machine is the only place that can.

echo_down:
        LDM   A, 0             ; T is the round trip. Latched into US first,
        STM   2, A             ; because the conversion overwrites dmem[0..1]
        LDM   A, 1
        STM   3, A
        LDI   A, 2
        STM   5, A
        JMP   convert

        ; =================================================================
        ; THE CONVERSION.  mm = us*11/64, exactly.
        ;   Q   = us >> 6        ACC = 11*(us & 63), then >> 6
        ;   T   = 11*(us >> 6)   T  += ACC, and T is the answer
        ; =================================================================
convert:
        ; ---- the small term: ACC = 11 * (us & 63) ---------------------
        LDM   A, 2
        AND   A, 0x3F
        STM   12, A
        LDI   A, 0
        STM   13, A            ; Q = r
        LDM   A, 12
        STM   0, A            ; ACC = Q, and THAT is where the chain starts:
        LDM   A, 13            ; x -> 2x -> 4x -> 5x -> 10x -> 11x is a chain
        STM   1, A            ; out of x, so an accumulator initialised to
        JMP   acc_x2           ; ZERO computes 3x instead of 11x and returns a
                               ; number, which is the worst kind of wrong.
acc_x2:                         ; ACC *= 2
        LDM   X, 0
        LDI   A, 0x80
        AND   A, X             ; bit 7 of the ORIGINAL low byte IS the carry
        JNZ   acc_x2_c
        LDM   X, 1
        LDM   A, 1
        ADD   A, X
        STM   1, A
        JMP   acc_x2_e
acc_x2_c:
        LDM   X, 1
        LDM   A, 1
        ADD   A, X
        ADD   A, 1
        STM   1, A
acc_x2_e:
        LDM   X, 0
        LDM   A, 0
        ADD   A, X
        STM   0, A
        JMP   acc_x4
acc_x4:                         ; ACC *= 2  ->  4r
        LDM   X, 0
        LDI   A, 0x80
        AND   A, X
        JNZ   acc_x4_c
        LDM   X, 1
        LDM   A, 1
        ADD   A, X
        STM   1, A
        JMP   acc_x4_e
acc_x4_c:
        LDM   X, 1
        LDM   A, 1
        ADD   A, X
        ADD   A, 1
        STM   1, A
acc_x4_e:
        LDM   X, 0
        LDM   A, 0
        ADD   A, X
        STM   0, A
        JMP   acc_x5
acc_x5:                         ; ACC += Q    ->  5r, a GENERAL add
LDM   A, 0
MOV   Y, A            ; dst_lo: never re-read, so the store over
                       ; it costs nothing
LDM   X, 12
ADD   A, X            ; sum
STM   0, A
STM   6, A             ; the sum, in the ANSWER SLOT: with ONE answer per run
                       ; the slot is unwritten for the whole conversion, and
                       ; dmem[2],dmem[3] (the width) and dmem[11] (the tick's
                       ; previous reading) are the only other bytes nothing
                       ; else reads while a chain runs
MOV   A, Y
AND   A, X            ; src & dst
STM   7, A
MOV   A, Y
OR    A, X            ; src | dst
STM   11, A
LDM   X, 6
LDI   A, 255
SUB   A, X            ; ~sum
LDM   X, 11
AND   A, X            ; (src|dst) & ~sum
STM   6, A             ; c2, written OVER the sum: the sum is finished with
                       ; by now, and three temporaries is what fits in a map
                       ; that also holds a banked answer
LDM   A, 7             ; c1
LDM   X, 6             ; c2
OR    A, X            ; the carry is BIT 7 of that byte: a carry out of an
                       ; add does NOT require a zero result
AND   A, 0x80
JZ    acc_x5_h
LDM   A, 1
ADD   A, 1
STM   1, A
acc_x5_h:
        LDM   X, 13
        LDM   A, 1
        ADD   A, X
        STM   1, A
        JMP   acc_x10
acc_x10:                        ; ACC *= 2  ->  10r
        LDM   X, 0
        LDI   A, 0x80
        AND   A, X
        JNZ   acc_x10_c
        LDM   X, 1
        LDM   A, 1
        ADD   A, X
        STM   1, A
        JMP   acc_x10_e
acc_x10_c:
        LDM   X, 1
        LDM   A, 1
        ADD   A, X
        ADD   A, 1
        STM   1, A
acc_x10_e:
        LDM   X, 0
        LDM   A, 0
        ADD   A, X
        STM   0, A
        JMP   acc_x11
acc_x11:                        ; ACC += Q    ->  11r
LDM   A, 0
MOV   Y, A            ; dst_lo: never re-read, so the store over
                       ; it costs nothing
LDM   X, 12
ADD   A, X            ; sum
STM   0, A
STM   6, A             ; the sum, in the ANSWER SLOT: with ONE answer per run
                       ; the slot is unwritten for the whole conversion, and
                       ; dmem[2],dmem[3] (the width) and dmem[11] (the tick's
                       ; previous reading) are the only other bytes nothing
                       ; else reads while a chain runs
MOV   A, Y
AND   A, X            ; src & dst
STM   7, A
MOV   A, Y
OR    A, X            ; src | dst
STM   11, A
LDM   X, 6
LDI   A, 255
SUB   A, X            ; ~sum
LDM   X, 11
AND   A, X            ; (src|dst) & ~sum
STM   6, A             ; c2, written OVER the sum: the sum is finished with
                       ; by now, and three temporaries is what fits in a map
                       ; that also holds a banked answer
LDM   A, 7             ; c1
LDM   X, 6             ; c2
OR    A, X            ; the carry is BIT 7 of that byte: a carry out of an
                       ; add does NOT require a zero result
AND   A, 0x80
JZ    acc_x11_h
LDM   A, 1
ADD   A, 1
STM   1, A
acc_x11_h:
        LDM   X, 13
        LDM   A, 1
        ADD   A, X
        STM   1, A

        ; ---- ACC >>= 6, the term: 11r is at most 693, so the result is
        ; ---- at most 10 and fits in ONE byte with a zero high byte.
        LDM   A, 0
        SHR   A
        SHR   A
        SHR   A
        SHR   A
        SHR   A
        SHR   A
        STM   0, A            ; (acc_lo >> 6) | ((acc_hi & 0x0f) << 2)
        LDM   A, 1
        AND   A, 0x0F          ; the bits that survive, then a LEFT shift by
        MOV   X, A             ; two -- which is two DOUBLINGS, because this
        ADD   A, X             ; ISA has SHR and no SHL. The first version of
        MOV   X, A             ; these two lines emulated the left shift with
        ADD   A, X             ; a right shift and two adds, which is not the
        LDM   X, 0            ; same operation: it gave (acc_hi>>2)+2 where
        OR    A, X             ; (acc_hi&0x0f)<<2 was meant, and a 1160 us echo
        STM   0, A            ; came out as 779 mm instead of 199.
        LDI   A, 0
        STM   1, A

        ; ---- Q = us >> 6, across the byte boundary -------------------
        ; A 16-bit shift right by six, in bytes. SHR is one bit at a time and
        ; there is no rotate, so the six bits that leave the top of the low
        ; byte are put back at the bottom of the high byte, which is what
        ; the OR is for. A shift LEFT by two of the high byte, computed as
        ; two shifts and two adds, is the same thing seen from the other
        ; end and is one instruction shorter than the shifts it replaces.
        LDM   A, 2
        SHR   A
        SHR   A
        SHR   A
        SHR   A
        SHR   A
        SHR   A
        STM   12, A
        LDM   A, 3
        SHR   A
        SHR   A
        SHR   A
        SHR   A
        SHR   A
        SHR   A                ; SIX shifts, not two: a 16-bit >>6 shifts
        STM   13, A            ; BOTH bytes by six, and the high byte was
                               ; written with two. That made Q 274 where it
                               ; should have been 18, and a 1160 us echo came
                               ; out as 822 mm -- a plausible millimetre
                               ; figure, which is the whole problem with this
                               ; act: nothing about a wrong answer here looks
                               ; wrong.
        LDM   A, 3
        AND   A, 0x3F          ; (us_hi & 0x3f), then a LEFT shift by two as
        MOV   X, A             ; two doublings -- the same correction as the
        ADD   A, X             ; small term's shift above, and the same reason
        MOV   X, A             ; to write it down twice: there is no SHL here
        ADD   A, X             ; and emulating one from SHR is wrong in a way
        LDM   X, 12            ; that still returns a number
        OR    A, X
        STM   12, A

        ; ---- ACC = 11 * (us >> 6): the same five steps, one accumulator
        ; away. T is dead from the falling edge onward, which is the only
        ; reason there is a second accumulator at all.
        LDM   A, 12
        STM   14, A             ; T = Q, for the same reason ACC = Q above
        LDM   A, 13
        STM   15, A
        JMP   t_x2
t_x2:
        LDM   X, 14
        LDI   A, 0x80
        AND   A, X
        JNZ   t_x2_c
        LDM   X, 15
        LDM   A, 15
        ADD   A, X
        STM   15, A
        JMP   t_x2_e
t_x2_c:
        LDM   X, 15
        LDM   A, 15
        ADD   A, X
        ADD   A, 1
        STM   15, A
t_x2_e:
        LDM   X, 14
        LDM   A, 14
        ADD   A, X
        STM   14, A
        JMP   t_x4
t_x4:
        LDM   X, 14
        LDI   A, 0x80
        AND   A, X
        JNZ   t_x4_c
        LDM   X, 15
        LDM   A, 15
        ADD   A, X
        STM   15, A
        JMP   t_x4_e
t_x4_c:
        LDM   X, 15
        LDM   A, 15
        ADD   A, X
        ADD   A, 1
        STM   15, A
t_x4_e:
        LDM   X, 14
        LDM   A, 14
        ADD   A, X
        STM   14, A
        JMP   t_x5
t_x5:                            ; T += Q, a general add with the guard
LDM   A, 14
MOV   Y, A            ; dst_lo: never re-read, so the store over
                       ; it costs nothing
LDM   X, 12
ADD   A, X            ; sum
STM   14, A
STM   6, A             ; the sum, in the ANSWER SLOT: with ONE answer per run
                       ; the slot is unwritten for the whole conversion, and
                       ; dmem[2],dmem[3] (the width) and dmem[11] (the tick's
                       ; previous reading) are the only other bytes nothing
                       ; else reads while a chain runs
MOV   A, Y
AND   A, X            ; src & dst
STM   7, A
MOV   A, Y
OR    A, X            ; src | dst
STM   11, A
LDM   X, 6
LDI   A, 255
SUB   A, X            ; ~sum
LDM   X, 11
AND   A, X            ; (src|dst) & ~sum
STM   6, A             ; c2, written OVER the sum: the sum is finished with
                       ; by now, and three temporaries is what fits in a map
                       ; that also holds a banked answer
LDM   A, 7             ; c1
LDM   X, 6             ; c2
OR    A, X            ; the carry is BIT 7 of that byte: a carry out of an
                       ; add does NOT require a zero result
AND   A, 0x80
JZ    t_x5_h
LDM   A, 15
ADD   A, 1
STM   15, A
t_x5_h:
        LDM   X, 13
        LDM   A, 15
        ADD   A, X
        STM   15, A
        JMP   t_x10
t_x10:
        LDM   X, 14
        LDI   A, 0x80
        AND   A, X
        JNZ   t_x10_c
        LDM   X, 15
        LDM   A, 15
        ADD   A, X
        STM   15, A
        JMP   t_x10_e
t_x10_c:
        LDM   X, 15
        LDM   A, 15
        ADD   A, X
        ADD   A, 1
        STM   15, A
t_x10_e:
        LDM   X, 14
        LDM   A, 14
        ADD   A, X
        STM   14, A
        JMP   t_x11
t_x11:
LDM   A, 14
MOV   Y, A            ; dst_lo: never re-read, so the store over
                       ; it costs nothing
LDM   X, 12
ADD   A, X            ; sum
STM   14, A
STM   6, A             ; the sum, in the ANSWER SLOT: with ONE answer per run
                       ; the slot is unwritten for the whole conversion, and
                       ; dmem[2],dmem[3] (the width) and dmem[11] (the tick's
                       ; previous reading) are the only other bytes nothing
                       ; else reads while a chain runs
MOV   A, Y
AND   A, X            ; src & dst
STM   7, A
MOV   A, Y
OR    A, X            ; src | dst
STM   11, A
LDM   X, 6
LDI   A, 255
SUB   A, X            ; ~sum
LDM   X, 11
AND   A, X            ; (src|dst) & ~sum
STM   6, A             ; c2, written OVER the sum: the sum is finished with
                       ; by now, and three temporaries is what fits in a map
                       ; that also holds a banked answer
LDM   A, 7             ; c1
LDM   X, 6             ; c2
OR    A, X            ; the carry is BIT 7 of that byte: a carry out of an
                       ; add does NOT require a zero result
AND   A, 0x80
JZ    t_x11_h
LDM   A, 15
ADD   A, 1
STM   15, A
t_x11_h:
        LDM   X, 13
        LDM   A, 15
        ADD   A, X
        STM   15, A

        ; ---- ACC += T, and ACC is the answer in millimetres --------
        LDM   X, 0
        LDM   A, 14
        ADD   A, X
        STM   14, A
        JNZ   fin_hi
        LDM   A, 0
        JZ    fin_hi
        LDM   A, 15
        ADD   A, 1
        STM   15, A
fin_hi:
        LDM   X, 1
        LDM   A, 15
        ADD   A, X
        STM   15, A

        ; ---- bank: ONE answer, low byte first, then park --------------------
        ; The address is 6, not 6 + 2*N, because this program measures ONE
        ; distance per run: that is what frees the answer slots for the whole
        ; conversion. And LOW BYTE FIRST, which every other act in this block
        ; does and this act's own earlier bank did: the first version of this
        ; stored the high byte first, and the testbench -- which reads
        ; little-endian -- reported 256 for an answer of 1.
        LDM   A, 14
        STM   6, A
        LDM   A, 15
        STM   7, A
        LDI   A, 1
        STM   10, A            ; DONE
park:
        JMP   park
